WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.414 --> 00:00:04.372
- [Narrator] Covering the
mountainside in Maras, Peru,

00:00:04.372 --> 00:00:08.076
thousands of manmade ponds
sit exposed to the elements,

00:00:08.076 --> 00:00:11.459
as they have for over 500 years.

00:00:11.459 --> 00:00:14.651
(inspiring guitar music)

00:00:14.651 --> 00:00:17.741
Beneath the Qaqawinay mountain,
where these salt pans sit,

00:00:17.741 --> 00:00:20.840
runs a salt-filled underground spring.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.181
The pans, each no more than a foot deep,

00:00:23.181 --> 00:00:25.459
fill with this natural spring water,

00:00:25.459 --> 00:00:27.043
which the sun evaporates,

00:00:27.043 --> 00:00:31.123
leaving behind the natural salt crystals.

00:00:31.123 --> 00:00:33.821
Created between 500 and 1000 A.D.,

00:00:33.821 --> 00:00:38.659
it was the Incans who built
them up into what we see today.

00:00:38.659 --> 00:00:41.240
Currently there are
over 3,000 active pans,

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.723
each one owned by a different
family in the Maras community,

00:00:44.723 --> 00:00:46.312
and are hand-mined much the same

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:48.360
as when the Incans were here.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.181
Each pool yields about 330
pounds of salt per month.

00:00:53.181 --> 00:00:55.683
When the salt has been
mined, the pool is refilled

00:00:55.683 --> 00:00:58.558
and the process begins again.

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:00.255
Visiting these pans allows you to marvel

00:01:00.255 --> 00:01:04.255
at the beauty of nature,
and of man's ingenuity.

